      "filename": "Eskişehir Meerschaum Museum in 2012 1644.jpg",
      "title": "File:Eskişehir Meerschaum Museum in 2012 1644.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Eski%C5%9Fehir_Meerschaum_Museum_in_2012_1644.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eski%C5%9Fehir_Meerschaum_Museum_in_2012_1644.jpg",
      "width": 4928,
      "height": 3280,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Dosseman",
      "description": "In part of the Kurşunlu Külliye is housed a museum showing the towns specialty, Lületaş, elsewhere known with the German word Meerschaum, a magnesium and silicon-based mineral, that is both lightweight and porous and moisture-absorbing. It seems to be ideal for the making of pipes, but can be worked into all sorts of objects. The region some 30 km southeast of Eskişehir has the largest deposits of meerschaum in the world.",
      "dateCreated": "2012-06-14 16:05:14",
      "sourceType": "poi",
      "sourceArticleId": "eskisehir"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Eskisehir Odunpazarı tour in 2018 8602.jpg",
      "title": "File:Eskisehir Odunpazarı tour in 2018 8602.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cb/Eskisehir_Odunpazar%C4%B1_tour_in_2018_8602.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eskisehir_Odunpazar%C4%B1_tour_in_2018_8602.jpg",
      "width": 4928,
      "height": 3280,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Dosseman",
      "description": "From a first visit I have always admired the town for seemingly considering its “old” houses not as “things of the past” but “things to be kept for the future”. During later visits many had not been torn down, but restored, in particular in an area called Odunpazarı (“Firewood market”). In 2018 I walked that specific region, paying attention to the old houses, many now splendidly restored, but also to the renovation works themselves and the contrasting “modern” buildings present also. In addition I visited some local artisans and took pictures of things that struck my fancy.",
      "dateCreated": "2018-10-17 15:20:42",
      "sourceType": "poi",
      "sourceArticleId": "eskisehir"
    },
